<h1>Introduction</h1>

In this tutorial we deal with finite strain elasticity using automatic 
differentiation (AD).

Finite strain elasticity is inherently nonlinear. This means that the 
deformations are large, and therefore, small strain theory fails. The 
governing equations must be formulated in terms of nonlinear kinematics 
and nonlinear stress-strain relationships. This results in a problem 
for which linear solvers are insufficient. We now require iterative 
Netwon methods.

This program deals with compressible neo-Hookean materials. Neo-Hookean 
materials have already been discussed in step-44; they are a type of 
hyperelastic materials. For hyperelastic materials we cannot longer 
say that the stress is proportional to the strain. Instead, the 
constitutive response can be derived from an elastic free energy 
potential. These constitutive models are often used for materials which 
experience large, i.e. nonlinear, elastic deformation.

Compressible neo-Hookean solid materials have a strain-energy function 
which is split into an isochoric and a volumetric part. This allows 
the resistance of the material to shape changes and volume changes 
to be controlled independently. Theoretically, this is a rather simple 
nonlinear hyperelastic material model. Therefore, we can use this 
material to focus on the numerical formulation of the tutorial 
instead of primarily on the material complexity, while still capturing 
the essential nonlinear behavior that is required to demonstrate 
finite strain effects.

The key challenge of this tutorial is how to correctly and efficiently 
compute the linearization of the residual. Doing this by hand could 
cause a lot of errors and would be very tedious. Hence, this program 
uses automatic differentiation (AD). AD simplifies this process and 
makes the implementation of nonlinear finite element methods (FEM) 
robust. With the help of AD, derivatives are computed exactly up to 
machine precision.

To demonstrate the effect of AD, the program implements three different 
assembly methods:
1. A fully manual approach: Residual and linearization are computed manually.
2. A hybrid approach: Residual is computed manually, while the linearization is performed using AD.
3. A method using only AD for both the computation of the residual and the linearization.